Moreover, in applications where evaluating $f$ is significantly faster than generating Gaussian random vectors, we can remove the space-time L´evy area $H _ { k }$ from (5.8). The resulting “increment-only” Shifted Euler method will then have the same cost per step as the traditional Euler-Maruyama method. However, for suficiently small $h ,$ it will still be more accurate as it gives $\begin{array} { r } { \frac { 1 } { 2 } h W _ { k } \approx \int _ { t _ { k } } ^ { t _ { k + 1 } } W _ { t _ { k } , r } } \end{array}$ dr in its Taylor expansion.

We now present our numerical example; a scalar stochastic anharmonic oscillator.

$$
d y _ {t} = \sin (y _ {t}) d t + d W _ {t}, \quad (y _ {0} = 1, T = 1)\tag{5.9}
$$

We compare our approaches (high order method (5.6) and low order method (5.8)) against the $3 / 2$ strong order SRA1 scheme in [75] and the Euler-Maruyama method:

$$
\begin{array}{l} Y _ {k + 1} ^ {\mathrm{A1}} := Y _ {k} ^ {\mathrm{A1}} + \frac {1}{3} f \big (Y _ {k} ^ {\mathrm{A1}} \big) h + \frac {2}{3} f \bigg (Y _ {k} ^ {\mathrm{A1}} + \frac {3}{4} \Big (f \big (Y _ {k} ^ {\mathrm{A1}} \big) h + \sigma (W _ {k} + 2 H _ {k}) \Big) \bigg) h + \sigma W _ {k}, \\ Y _ {k + 1} ^ {\mathrm{EM}} := Y _ {k} ^ {\mathrm{EM}} + f \big (Y _ {k} ^ {\mathrm{EM}} \big) h + \sigma W _ {k}. \end{array}
$$

We use the non-linear splitting path (4.8) to obtain the variables $C _ { 1 } , C _ { 2 }$ in (5.6).

As the Shifted Ralston and Euler methods have the same orders of convergence as the SRA1 and Euler-Maruyama methods, we estimate the ratios of their $L ^ { 2 } ( \mathbb { P } )$ errors. Our results are presented in Figure 5, where we observe that the proposed methods achieve roughly a 3× improvement in their accuracy for suficiently small step sizes.

